  "source_text": "Part Four. The Fall.\n\nChapter Fifty Five.\n\nAt 12:17 a.m. on Monday, Adrian Marsh was still staring at the phone. Seb had missed the 11:55 p.m. check-in. That was what Adrian would remember later: the way the worst news arrived with a silence where Seb's voice should have been. The burner sat on the scarred wooden table of a rented flat in Zurich. Nadia had reached the city ahead of him; he had come through Dusseldorf on a flight booked under a reserve identity.\n\nThree minutes late could mean traffic. Five minutes could mean caution. Ten minutes meant Seb was running.\n\nTwenty minutes meant Seb was dead.\n\nAdrian stared at the phone as if will alone could make it vibrate. Around him, the safehouse smelled of stale coffee and old wallpaper paste. A crack ran through the ceiling plaster like a river delta, like one of Priya's network maps, he thought, and then hated himself for thinking it.\n\nAt twenty-two minutes, Nadia's burner buzzed instead. She answered, listened, and the color drained from her face like water from a cracked vessel.\n\n\"They got him,\" she said. \"Seb's gone.\"\n\nThe words landed in the room like a grenade with the pin already pulled. Adrian felt his chest compress, felt the air leave the space as if someone had opened an airlock. He gripped the edge of the table with both hands.\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"The farmhouse. A courier found him in the upstairs room. Gunshot.\"\n\nThe farmhouse. The place they had cleared for the last meeting. He saw the coffee pot, the oil lamp, the muddy road beyond the doorway. Seb had stayed behind to finish the contingency instructions while the others moved toward Zurich.\n\nAdrian closed his eyes. Four hours earlier he had been sitting across from Seb at that table. The older man had looked tired. Not the performative exhaustion of the overworked activist but the bone-deep fatigue of someone who knew what the next decision might cost.\n\n\"If something happens to me,\" Seb had said, \"the operation continues.\"\n\nAdrian had tried to stop him. Seb had repeated the instruction until he listened.\n\nNow Seb was gone too.\n\nPriya. Then the cell members exposed by the AI hack. Now the man who had started it all.\n\nThe movement wasn't just wounded. It was hemorrhaging.\n\nAdrian opened his eyes. Nadia was watching him with that journalist's gaze, cataloging his reaction, filing it, already composing the narrative. Not out of coldness. Out of survival instinct. She processed the world by turning it into story, the same way he processed it through ethical frameworks. Right now both methods felt spectacularly useless.\n\nGrief was — no. Not now. He could feel it pulling at him, the undertow of it, wanting him to sit with it, to think it through the way he thought everything through. But Seb wouldn't have sat. Seb would have been making calls already. Seb would have been three steps ahead, assigning roles, checking timelines, turning loss into momentum.\n\nSo that's what Adrian would do. All of it. Everything Seb would have done, because Seb couldn't do it anymore, and because the only framework that mattered now was the one that kept the people in this room alive and moving.\n\nThe grief could wait only if he kept moving. He opened his notebook to the instructions Seb had dictated.\n\n\"We have to tell the others,\" Adrian said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"And we have to keep moving. The window —\"\n\n\"I know, Adrian.\"\n\nThe 48-hour window. The zero-day attack the AI had fired as its dying act, a parting gift from their murdered digital guardian, forcing every oligarch company into crisis mode. Engineers at six of the most powerful corporations in the world were currently scrambling to patch exploits they didn't yet understand, their attention turned inward, their surveillance apparatus running at reduced capacity.\n\nIt was the opening Seb had been waiting for.\n\nAnd Seb would never walk through it.",
